<section class="formula">
  <h2>Percentage Formulas</h2>
  <p><strong>What is X% of Y:</strong> Answer = (X / 100) x Y</p>
  <p>Example: What is 15% of 200? (15 / 100) x 200 = 30</p>
  <p><strong>Percentage change:</strong> Change% = ((New - Old) / Old) x 100</p>
  <p>Example: Price rises from $40 to $50. Change = ((50 - 40) / 40) x 100 = 25% increase</p>
  <p><strong>X is what % of Y:</strong> Answer = (X / Y) x 100</p>
  <p>Example: 45 is what percent of 180? (45 / 180) x 100 = 25%</p>
  <p>These three formulas cover the vast majority of percentage problems encountered in everyday life, school, and work.</p>
</section>

<section class="examples">
  <h2>Percentage Examples</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Discount at a store:</strong> A jacket costs $120 and is 30% off. What do you pay? 30% of 120 = 36. Final price = $120 - $36 = $84.</li>
    <li><strong>Pay raise:</strong> Your salary was $52,000 and you got a 4% raise. New salary = 4% of 52,000 = $2,080 extra. New total = $54,080.</li>
    <li><strong>Exam score:</strong> You got 68 out of 80 questions right. 68 is what percent of 80? (68/80) x 100 = 85%.</li>
    <li><strong>Sales growth:</strong> Last month revenue was $15,000, this month it is $18,750. % change = ((18750-15000)/15000) x 100 = 25% growth.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

