<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <h3>What is 20% of 150?</h3>
  <p>20% of 150 is 30. You multiply 150 by 0.20 to get the answer. You can use the first mode in this calculator to confirm any similar problem instantly.</p>

  <h3>How do I calculate percentage change?</h3>
  <p>Percentage change is ((new value minus old value) divided by old value) times 100. If a price goes from $80 to $100 that is a 25% increase. If it drops from $100 to $80 that is a 20% decrease. The direction matters. A rise from 80 to 100 and a fall from 100 to 80 are not the same percentage.</p>

  <h3>What is the difference between percentage and percentage points?</h3>
  <p>Percentage describes a proportion relative to a base. Percentage points describe the arithmetic difference between two percentages. If interest rates go from 2% to 5% that is a 3 percentage point increase but a 150% percentage change. The two are very different and mixing them up is a common source of confusion in news reporting.</p>

  <h3>How do I find what percent one number is of another?</h3>
  <p>Divide the part by the whole then multiply by 100. For example: 30 is what percent of 120? Divide 30 by 120 to get 0.25, then multiply by 100 to get 25%.</p>

  <h3>Can I calculate a reverse percentage?</h3>
  <p>Yes. If you know the final value after a percentage increase and want to find the original, divide by (1 + rate). For a 25% increase where the final price is $125, divide by 1.25 to get the original $100. For a decrease, divide by (1 minus rate).</p>
</section>

